        body:['Readiness is not a single technology score. A team may have excellent models and weak source control, or mature content operations and no representative evaluation set. Assess the system dimension by dimension.','Score what happens most of the time, not what the process document says should happen. Evidence includes content samples, system behavior, ownership records, review data, retrieval logs, and observed decisions.'],
        workflow:['Select one bounded use case and its content domain.','Sample sources for duplication, age, authority, structure, and ownership.','Interview the people who create, approve, retrieve, and use the knowledge.','Run representative questions and trace failures by layer.','Rank interventions by risk reduction, user value, and effort.'],
        example:{title:'Readiness is uneven',content:'A developer portal has strong templates and metadata but weak ownership after product reorganization. The first investment is not a new search engine; it is restoring accountable owners and review rules for the highest-traffic content.'},
        lab:{title:'Run a 30-minute evidence audit',task:'Review ten content items from one domain. Record owner, status, last review, audience, product/version, authoritative source, and whether the item should be AI-eligible.',deliverable:'A readiness table with the top three systemic gaps and one recommended pilot boundary.'},
        checklist:['Assessment is scoped to a use case','Scores cite observable evidence','Variations between teams are visible','Risks and business value are considered together','Recommendations are sequenced, not presented as a wish list'],
        check:{q:'Which evidence gives the most reliable readiness signal?',options:['A future-state strategy deck','A tool vendor demonstration','A sample of content and observed workflow decisions','The number of AI projects proposed'],answer:2,explanation:'Readiness should reflect current operating reality. Samples and observed decisions reveal whether controls actually work.'}
