        summary:'Turn pages and documents into purposeful content types with fields, constraints, and relationships.',
        objectives:['Separate meaning from presentation','Define content types around user intent','Model reuse without fragmenting comprehension'],
        concepts:[['Content type','A repeatable semantic structure such as concept, procedure, reference, claim, policy, or troubleshooting item.'],['Field','A named unit with a purpose, format, requirement, and validation rule.'],['Relationship','A typed connection such as procedure uses API, claim supported by evidence, or policy applies to market.'],['Assembly','A rule for combining reusable objects into a coherent experience for a context.']],
        body:['AI-ready content is not simply shorter content. It is knowledge whose purpose and context are explicit enough to retrieve, validate, assemble, and govern.','Start with user tasks and business rules. Model only the distinctions that change retrieval, reuse, review, or delivery. Excessive granularity creates authoring burden; page-level blobs hide the context machines need.'],
        workflow:['Inventory representative content and recurring user intents.','Identify stable semantic types and the fields each requires.','Define relationships, validation rules, and ownership.','Test the model against authoring, retrieval, localization, and change scenarios.','Pilot with real content before scaling the schema.'],
        example:{title:'Procedure model',content:'A procedure can require: goal, prerequisites, ordered steps, expected result, troubleshooting links, product/version, audience, permissions, owner, review date, and related concepts. Presentation can vary while meaning remains stable.'},
        lab:{title:'Model one support journey',task:'Choose a concept, procedure, and troubleshooting article that support the same user task. Define shared fields, type-specific fields, and relationships among them.',deliverable:'A lightweight model with field definitions and one assembled user journey.'},
        checklist:['Each type has a distinct user or system purpose','Required context is represented as data','Relationships are named and directional','Writers can author the model without hidden work','The model improves at least two downstream uses'],
        check:{q:'What is the best reason to split content into a separate type?',options:['The page is long','A different team wrote it','It has a distinct purpose, lifecycle, or reuse pattern','The CMS supports more schemas'],answer:2,explanation:'A content type should represent a meaningful difference in purpose, governance, validation, or reuse—not arbitrary length or ownership.'}
