        concepts:[['Taxonomy','A governed vocabulary and classification structure used to describe a domain consistently.'],['Metadata','Structured attributes that describe content, context, status, relationships, and use.'],['Entity','A stable real-world object—product, audience, capability, policy, market, or task—with an identity.'],['Facet','An independent classification dimension users or systems can combine as filters.']],
        body:['Metadata earns its cost only when it powers a decision: what to retrieve, who should review, where to publish, when to retire, or how to measure.','Begin with retrieval and governance questions. If a field does not change behavior, reporting, or accountability, it may not belong in the first model. Controlled values need definitions, examples, owners, and a change process.'],
        workflow:['List the questions search, AI, publishing, and governance must answer.','Translate those questions into the smallest useful set of fields.','Define allowed values, definitions, aliases, and examples.','Assign vocabulary owners and change rules.','Measure completion, accuracy, and downstream use—not field count.'],
        example:{title:'Metadata for technical guidance',content:'Product, version, audience, task, content type, status, source authority, owner, effective date, review date, risk tier, and AI eligibility can jointly prevent wrong-version retrieval and route the right review.'},
        lab:{title:'Create a retrieval filter model',task:'Take five plausible user questions. For each, identify the metadata filters needed before semantic similarity is considered.',deliverable:'A table mapping questions to product, version, audience, task, authority, and risk filters.'},
        checklist:['Every required field supports a named decision','Values have definitions and examples','Synonyms and aliases are handled','Ownership and update process are explicit','Metadata quality is measured at the point of use'],
        check:{q:'Which metadata field most directly prevents an old answer from being treated as current?',options:['Word count','Author biography','Effective date and status','Page color'],answer:2,explanation:'Effective date and lifecycle status allow retrieval and governance rules to distinguish current from superseded knowledge.'}
