        concepts:[['Node','An identifiable entity such as a product, task, API, audience, claim, policy, or document.'],['Edge','A typed relationship such as supports, applies to, supersedes, requires, or contradicts.'],['Property','Context stored on a node or edge, such as date, version, confidence, status, or source.'],['Ontology','A shared model of entity types, relationship types, constraints, and domain meaning.']],
        body:['A knowledge graph is useful when answers depend on relationships that are difficult to recover reliably from isolated text. Examples include version dependencies, evidence chains, product compatibility, policy applicability, and ownership.','Design from questions, not diagrams. List the multi-step questions users must answer, then identify the entities and relationships required to traverse from question to evidence.'],
        workflow:['Collect high-value questions that require connections.','Identify stable entities and their unique identifiers.','Name directional relationships using precise verbs.','Attach provenance, status, time, and confidence where decisions require them.','Test paths against real questions and governance scenarios.'],
