        summary:'Trace ingestion, indexing, retrieval, context assembly, generation, citation, and feedback as one system.',
        objectives:['Explain every stage of a RAG system','Identify decisions hidden inside ingestion','Use telemetry to locate failure causes'],
        concepts:[['Ingestion','Selecting, parsing, cleaning, and transforming eligible source content.'],['Chunking','Creating retrievable units while preserving the context needed for interpretation.'],['Indexing','Representing content for lexical, semantic, structured, or graph retrieval.'],['Context assembly','Choosing and ordering evidence supplied to the model under a token budget.']],
        body:['RAG is not a single retrieval call. Every stage encodes policy: which sources enter, how they are segmented, what metadata survives, which candidates rank, and what evidence reaches generation.','A production pipeline should retain lineage from answer back to chunk, source, owner, version, and ingestion event. Without lineage, teams can observe a bad answer but cannot repair it efficiently.'],
        workflow:['Define eligible sources and ingestion rules.','Parse structure and preserve headings, tables, code, and relationships.','Create chunks with identifiers, metadata, and parent context.','Retrieve candidates with filters and multiple signals.','Assemble evidence, generate with constraints, cite, evaluate, and log.'],
