        concepts:[['Eligibility filter','A hard rule that determines whether content may be considered at all.'],['Lexical signal','Exact term, identifier, error code, API name, or phrase overlap.'],['Semantic signal','Meaning similarity between a query and content representation.'],['Reranking','A second-stage comparison of candidates using richer context and criteria.']],
        body:['Similarity is not authority. A highly similar draft, deprecated page, or wrong-market policy should not outrank approved knowledge. Use filters to establish eligibility before ranking relevance.','Hybrid retrieval combines signals because user questions vary. Error codes benefit from exact matching; paraphrased concepts benefit from semantic retrieval; product compatibility may require graph traversal; policy answers require source tier and effective date.'],
        workflow:['Classify representative query types.','Define hard eligibility rules for status, access, version, market, and risk.','Choose lexical, semantic, graph, and behavioral signals by query type.','Retrieve broadly, then rerank with authority and context.','Measure recall, precision, evidence sufficiency, and downstream answer quality.'],
